# Dehaze — He, Sun & Tang's dark channel prior (2009).
#
# WHAT IT IS DOING, because the sliders make more sense once the model does.
#
# A hazy pixel is a mix of the real scene and scattered light:
#
#     I = J * t  +  A * (1 - t)
#
#   I  what the camera saw
#   J  the haze-free scene
#   A  the AIRLIGHT -- the colour of the haze, usually a pale grey-blue
#   t  TRANSMISSION -- how much of the scene survived, 1 clear and 0 pure haze
#
# t falls off with distance, so dehazing is really depth estimation: the "haze
# map" this recovers IS a rough depth map, which is why the correction grows
# toward the horizon on its own rather than needing a gradient painted in.
#
# One equation, two unknowns. The DARK CHANNEL PRIOR supplies the rest: in
# almost any patch of a haze-free outdoor photograph, some pixel is nearly black
# in some channel -- a shadow, dark foliage, a window. Where that local minimum
# is NOT dark, the brightness was added by haze, and how much tells you t.
#
# PUT IT BEFORE THE TONE CURVE. This inverts a physical mixing of light, so it
# belongs in scene-linear values. Applied to display values it lifts the shadows
# instead and the picture washes out.

src = image("test")

# STRENGTH is how much of the estimated haze to remove.
#
# Not 1.0, even though the model would allow it: removing all the haze takes
# away the aerial perspective that tells the eye something is distant, and a
# fully dehazed landscape reads as a flat cutout. He uses 0.95 on deliberately
# hazy scenes; 0.7-0.9 is the useful range for a photograph.
amount = slider("dehaze", 0, 1, 0.8,
                "How much of the estimated haze to remove. 0 is off.")

# SKY PROTECT covers the prior's known failure.
#
# The prior assumes every patch has a dark pixel in it. A large bright region
# with none -- open sky, snow, a white wall -- looks exactly like haze, so it
# gets darkened and often turns grey. This floors the transmission where a pixel
# is already close to the airlight COLOUR, which leaves a white building against
# blue sky alone while protecting the sky itself.
#
# Set it to 0 to see what the raw prior does; the difference over the sky is
# usually the most visible thing this algorithm does.
protect = slider("sky protect", 0, 1, 0.8,
                 "Floors the transmission where a pixel is already the airlight colour, so sky and snow are not read as haze.")

hazy = src => display("original")

clear = dehaze(hazy, strength = amount, sky_protect = protect)
        => display("dehazed")

# THE TWO RADII are the ones worth understanding if the result looks wrong, and
# they fail in opposite directions. Both are stated per 1000 px and scaled to
# the image, so a value that works here works on a 45 MP frame too.
#
#   patch    the window the dark channel is minimised over. Too small and the
#            prior gets noisy, because a small patch may genuinely contain no
#            dark pixel. Too large and thin bright things against sky -- a bare
#            branch, a mast -- are swallowed and treated as haze.
#
#   refine   the guided filter that snaps the transmission map to the image's
#            edges. Too small and the map stays blocky, which shows as halos
#            wherever depth changes. Too large is the subtler failure: it
#            smooths ACROSS depth, so the far distance stops being corrected
#            more than the foreground and the whole effect flattens into a
#            contrast boost. Measured on a synthetic depth ramp, an oversized
#            refine radius cut the far-versus-near correction ratio from 2.7x
#            to 1.5x.
#
# Uncomment to expose them:
#
# _ = params(dehaze)

display(clear, "final")
